### IHostedService in Blazor WebAssembly

.NET 11 enables running `IHostedService` implementations in the browser via WebAssembly.

#### Registration (Program.cs)
```csharp
builder.Services.AddHostedService<BackgroundUpdateService>();
```

#### Service Implementation
```csharp
public class BackgroundUpdateService : IHostedService
{
    private readonly ILogger<BackgroundUpdateService> _logger;
    private Timer _timer;

    public BackgroundUpdateService(ILogger<BackgroundUpdateService> logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }

    public Task StartAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Background service starting");
        _timer = new Timer(DoWork, null, TimeSpan.Zero, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    private void DoWork(object state)
    {
        // Background work in browser context
    }

    public Task StopAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        _timer?.Change(Timeout.Infinite, 0);
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

This follows the minimal API approach with clear naming and focused methods.

# `UseKestrel(port)` silently ignores port parameter on derived `WebApplicationFactory` from `WithWebHostBuilder()`

## Summary

`WebApplicationFactory<T>.UseKestrel(int port)` correctly applies the port when called on the base factory or a subclass. However, the port parameter is silently ignored when called on a factory derived via `WithWebHostBuilder()`. The server falls back to the Kestrel default port of 5000.

`WithWebHostBuilder` is the documented approach for per-test service customization.

## Repro

```csharp
[Fact]
public async Task UseKestrel_WithSpecificPort_OnDerivedFactory_PortIsIgnored()
{
    var expectedPort = FindFreePort();
    await using var factory = new WebApplicationFactory<Program>()
        .WithWebHostBuilder(_ => { });
    factory.UseKestrel(expectedPort);

    var actualPort = GetServerPort(factory);
    Assert.Equal(expectedPort, actualPort); // fails
}
```

## Root Cause

The `DelegatedWebApplicationFactory` captures the parent's `CreateHost` delegate. `TryConfigureServerPort` executes in the context of the parent instance, reading its unset `_kestrelPort` field.

## Workaround

Use a subclass that overrides `ConfigureWebHost` rather than `WithWebHostBuilder`.

# Custom WebApplicationFactory for Reliable Port Configuration

## Implementation

```csharp
public class CustomWebApplicationFactory : WebApplicationFactory<Program>
{
    protected override void ConfigureWebHost(IWebHostBuilder builder)
    {
        builder.ConfigureServices(services =>
        {
            // Replace or add services as needed for the specific test
        });
    }
}
```

## Usage

```csharp
var expectedPort = FindFreePort();
await using var factory = new CustomWebApplicationFactory();
factory.UseKestrel(expectedPort);

var actualPort = GetServerPort(factory);
Assert.Equal(expectedPort, actualPort);
```

This approach ensures the factory's own `CreateHost` implementation is used, allowing `TryConfigureServerPort` to read the correct `_kestrelPort` value set by `UseKestrel`.

# Steps to Reproduce

1. Create a minimal web app using the WebApplication template.
2. Add an xunit test project referencing the app and `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing`.
3. Add the test below.
4. Run `dotnet test`.

```csharp
[Fact]
public async Task UseKestrel_WithSpecificPort_OnDerivedFactory_PortIsIgnored()
{
    var expectedPort = FindFreePort();
    await using var factory = new WebApplicationFactory<Program>()
        .WithWebHostBuilder(_ => { });
    factory.UseKestrel(expectedPort);

    var server = factory.Services.GetRequiredService<IServer>();
    var addresses = server.Features.Get<IServerAddressesFeature>()!.Addresses;
    var actualPort = new Uri(addresses.First()).Port;

    Assert.Equal(expectedPort, actualPort);
}
```

The test fails with port 5000 instead of the expected value. The equivalent test without `WithWebHostBuilder` passes.

# Server Port Utility

```csharp
public static class WebApplicationFactoryExtensions
{
    public static int GetPort<T>(this WebApplicationFactory<T> factory) where T : class
    {
        var server = factory.Services.GetRequiredService<IServer>();
        var addressFeature = server.Features.Get<IServerAddressesFeature>();
        var address = addressFeature?.Addresses?.FirstOrDefault();

        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(address))
        {
            throw new InvalidOperationException("No server address found.");
        }

        return new Uri(address).Port;
    }
}
```

## Usage

```csharp
await using var factory = new WebApplicationFactory<Program>();
factory.UseKestrel(0);

var port = factory.GetPort();
Console.WriteLine($"Server running on port {port}");
```

The utility should be called after the server has started. It isolates port retrieval logic for cleaner test code.

